[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm treating this as a bounded change: the page already exists and we're adding filters to it. So I'll ask a few
  questions here and then give you a short design in chat. There won't be a spec file, and I won't write any code until
  you've said yes to the design.

  Where the controls go is easier to judge by looking, so I've opened a browser tab with three rough wireframes:
  [94m]8;id=1qugmol;http://localhost:57395/?key=34aa717ab1820ab17956b209cc4bd01b95b585d9b54e03767949d7fb8dafd686\http://localhost:57395/?key=34aa717ab1820ab17956b209cc4bd01b95b585d9b54e03767949d7fb8dafd686[39m]8;;\ (it uses a fair number of
  tokens, so tell me if you'd rather stay in the terminal). The tab is open at the screen with the three options:

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table.[0m An event type Select, a date range preset, a "Failed sign-ins only" checkbox, a
    "Clear filters" link and a live "Showing X of Y events" count. Everything is visible all the time.
  - [1mB. A "Filter" button that opens a popover.[0m Active filters show as removable tags. The page stays tidier, but finding
    a failed sign-in takes two or three clicks.
  - [1mC. Quick-filter chips for groups of types.[0m This is the fastest to scan. It does mean defining groups of the nine
    event types, and no component in the UI guidelines fits it well.

  I recommend A. Security and the support tickets are both about spotting failed sign-ins, and A gets you there in one
  click. It also uses only components the guidelines already define: Select, a checkbox and a live region for the count.
  B is the better pick if you expect more filters later.

  Which placement do you want? You can click an option in the browser, but please answer here too.

  The mockups are saved under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the repo. The repo has no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m, so that folder shows up as
  untracked. I won't commit it, and I'll leave adding a [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m to you.

